WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.720
Every order needs its customer's loyalty tier, because the tier sets the cashback.

2
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:13.325
Calling the customer database for every order would mean 3,000 calls a second.

3
00:00:13.325 --> 00:00:19.616
So the join keeps a local copy: a table built from every tier change, one row per customer.

4
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.542
Customer c-1 is silver from 9:00.

5
00:00:22.542 --> 00:00:24.869
Its row goes into the table.

6
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:27.676
At 10:00, c-1 is upgraded to gold.

7
00:00:27.676 --> 00:00:31.437
The table overwrites the row, and silver is gone.

8
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:37.723
Order o-1 was placed at 9:30, but it sat in a partition whose consumer is behind.

9
00:00:37.723 --> 00:00:39.903
It reaches the join at 10:01.

10
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.087
The join looks up c-1 and finds gold, the only row it has.

11
00:00:44.087 --> 00:00:48.967
The order earns gold cashback for a purchase made while c-1 was silver.

12
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:55.427
Rebuild the output next week from the same topics, and the partitions may interleave the other way.

13
00:00:55.427 --> 00:00:58.716
Now o-1 arrives before the upgrade, and gets silver.

14
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.605
Same input, two answers.

15
00:01:00.605 --> 00:01:05.505
The result depends on arrival order, so the join isn't deterministic.

16
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.115
The fix is to keep the table's history.

17
00:01:08.115 --> 00:01:12.945
Make the table versioned: it stores each version with the time it took effect.

18
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:18.977
For o-1, the join now asks which version was valid at the order's own time, 9:30.

19
00:01:18.977 --> 00:01:20.997
That's silver, in every run.

20
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.519
Versions can't help when the table is the late one.

21
00:01:24.519 --> 00:01:31.027
If the upgrade arrives late, order o-2, placed at 10:02, gets there first and is joined with silver.

22
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:36.336
A grace period holds each order a while first, say 5 minutes of stream time.

23
00:01:36.336 --> 00:01:42.447
The upgrade arrives meanwhile, so o-2 gets gold, and every order comes out 5 minutes later.

24
00:01:42.667 --> 00:01:45.673
A plain table answers with the tier as it is now.

25
00:01:45.673 --> 00:01:50.765
To get the tier as it was, keep the history and look it up by the order's own time.
